WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.862 --> 00:00:03.816
[DING]

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.160
So reactlog is a memory
leak by definition.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.040
So we have to opt into it.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.470
And we can opt into it using
reactlog, reactlog enable.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.240
So I'll go ahead
and enable that now.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:28.480
And then we can go ahead and
run our Shiny application

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.030
no problem, just like
we're used to doing.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.930
And this one is the
old faithful example

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.950
that we're used to seeing
of all of the waiting times.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.970
And so we can change the bin
count and the plot updates.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.680
Great.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.770
We can then view the reactlog
by doing Command, fn, 3, for me.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:56.020
And it might be like option
or control, fn, 3 for Windows,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.480
I can't quite remember.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.720
But your reactlog will show
up in a separate window.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.590
And there's lots going on
here, but for just quick look

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:10.390
we can see our input bins
that we had on the left.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.880
And on the right, we
can see our output

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:20.580
of our distribution plot, a
dist plot from the application.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.810
And let's take a look.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:30.300
So the way that reactlog works
is it flows from left to right.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.710
And I made the pieces so that
they fit in like a puzzle.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.250
So you can have your
inputs here on the left,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.350
and they can join into your
reactive values in the middle.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.870
So it joins in there and
needs to be finished off

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.890
with an output value
on the far right.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.500
In this case, there is
a theme counter here,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.357
but that's not having
any outputs listen to it.

00:01:55.357 --> 00:01:57.190
So that's why it's all
the way on the right.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:01.770
So we kind of align right
on all of the values.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.990
But theme counter we can ignore.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:07.200
We're mainly just interested
in the output of the distPlot.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.270
The reactlog, if we
look, let's just kind of

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:14.440
walk through the top
of this status bar.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.040
So we have the eight
navigation buttons at the top.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.890
The first and last
are traversing

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.420
between marked steps.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.390
We haven't done that yet, and
we'll come back to that later.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.480
The next ones is
a tall green bar.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.700
That's this tall
green bar here, which

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.540
is when Shiny was idle for
at least a millisecond.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.240
It declared that it was idle.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.000
And then the short
green bar is an output.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.700
So an output calculation.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.040
And you can jump between
prior and previous outputs.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.910
So you may calculate
five outputs

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.170
and then Shiny becomes idle.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.020
So you may want
to jump in between

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.110
to see how your active updates.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.170
But the ones that I use a lot
are these middle two, which

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.450
is previous step and next step.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.050
And I don't like to use
the mouse that often,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:07.140
so we can actually use the
right arrow and left arrow.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.830
And you can hold it and
you can hold it back.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.620
And the KeyCastr is showing
that I was holding that there.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.520
So that's pretty exciting.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.160
Reactlog defaults
to the first time

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.560
that Shiny was idle
because it doesn't actually

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.510
know where you want to start.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:28.050
I find starting at the end was--

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.740
where I launched reactlog
was too far away.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.660
Because most likely,
I want to build up

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.610
into the unexpected error.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.970
Or build up into what I'm trying
to learn about the application.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:43.530
So I started it at the
first stable location.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.290
And stable is when most of
the outputs, or in this case,

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.670
all of the outputs and
reactive values are green.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.610
They are in the ready state.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.040
If they were not
in the ready state,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.830
and they're actually
performing some calculations,

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.150
they will be yellow.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.780
It's useful to see
these different states.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.540
In the future I'd actually
like to show more states,

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:10.960
like a red for an error occurred
or something like a wreck

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.190
false.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:15.640
But I was just trying
to show currently

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.290
what Shiny is aware of.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.709
Because technically, a req()
false is a valid result.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.440
And so it's not
calculating anything.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.220
It is actually ready to move
on even though the value is not

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.018
what we want.

00:04:28.018 --> 00:04:29.560
So it'd be better
in the future if we

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.235
could differentiate those.

00:04:31.235 --> 00:04:32.860
So now we're at an
idle, and let's take

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.170
a peek at what happens when--

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:39.520
Remember, I changed
the bins in the plot.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.830
So in the sidebar
I changed the--

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.780
This is where the
width actually changed.

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:52.720
Probably due to the
width of the plot

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.470
actually was changing probably
due to this little showcase

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:57.790
mode on the side.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:02.260
And so two times the plot was
redrawn because the output

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.505
width changed.

00:05:06.262 --> 00:05:07.720
So let's walk
actually through when

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.840
I change the int of the bins.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.360
And so I changed
it from 30 to 21.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:19.610
And the way Shiny works is
all downstream dependencies

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.110
will be invalidated.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.170
And they will remove
all connections

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:28.470
both up and downstream
from their connections.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.410
So it's not fully
invasive, but it

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.260
is invasive for everything
downstream of this input value.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.950
So let's walk through it.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.860
This will tell,
so the input bins

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.800
will say, hey all my
downstream listeners you now

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.030
should start invalidating.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:48.230
So plot output, or a plotObj
will start invalidating.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.020
He's going to tell of his
downstream dependencies which

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.470
is going to be the
output distPlot.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.920
Output distPlot says OK I
will start invalidating.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.870
They will start
invalidating, by removing,

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:01.010
all upstream dependencies.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.390
So the black lines are now
becoming thin little lines.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:08.440
The thin lines I kept in
there to say a connection

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.850
has occurred in this recording
but it is currently not there.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:14.800
Where if it's a thicker
line, then that means

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.930
it's actively a dependency.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.270
OK, distPlot has
removed all connections

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.090
and it is invalidated.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:28.330
So now we are backing out
of our invalidating process

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.480
and plotObj will remove
its last dependency

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.150
and is now finally invalidated.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.330
And then the input
value is done,

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.340
and it will just
automatically jump over

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:43.330
into the next step, which is
the output starts calculating.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:50.320
Shiny works in a model
where it finds all the data

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:52.570
that it needs to render.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.790
It is not necessarily
a push model.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:59.020
So I did not push input
bins into the plot object.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.620
It's actually the plot object
is reaching up and finding

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.078
input bins.

00:07:04.078 --> 00:07:06.370
It's a little different than
the way we think about it.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.490
It's because of the
two different steps

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:12.580
where the first part was input
bins tells all downstream

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.810
to invalidate.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.970
But then it's a free for all.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.000
Finally now output distPlot
can then work its way

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.030
upstream until it's satisfied.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.580
So output distPlot
starts calculating

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.670
says, hey, I need
the plot object.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.040
Plot object says, I
haven't calculated

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.840
so I need to start calculating.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.360
Then it says, well, I don't
have everything I need.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.260
I need a connection
to in this case,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.040
it's these unexpected
three items here.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.260
The client data output distPlot
width, client data output

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.430
distPlot height, and also
client data pixel ratio.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:53.990
These three pieces
of information

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:57.820
are necessary to draw
an image in Shiny.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:02.430
And I think it's really neat,
because if you squeeze the plot

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.930
we should redraw it because it
doesn't really look like it's

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.280
going to fill the proper area.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:09.670
If you make the
plot taller we want

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:12.820
to redraw it so your output
height and width will change.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.830
And then your pixel
ratio is, if you actually

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.230
do like command plus
or command minus

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.650
and you go to retina display
or non retina display,

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:24.740
the quality of the image
will need to be adjusted.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:27.410
And so in this case,
plotObj is going

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:31.220
to be retrieving those
values via an isolate.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.270
That's why it has a dashed line.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.563
So it's isolating on the value.

00:08:35.563 --> 00:08:36.980
And then it removes
the connection

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:41.360
because an isolation does
not have any reactivity.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.800
And then we get the pixel
ratio value and the input bins.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.800
At this point plotObj said,
hey, I got all my values,

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.090
I'm calculated.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.980
So we can go back
to the distPlot

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.500
and continue the calculations.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.910
In this case distPlot does
have a hard dependency

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.530
on the output distPlot
width, the height,

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.983
and the pixel ratio.

00:09:08.983 --> 00:09:10.900
Mentally thinking, I
thought it would actually

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.658
be inside the
plotObj but it just

00:09:12.658 --> 00:09:14.200
happens to be in
the output distPlot.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:21.060
distPlot is now satisfied,
and it is done calculating.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.400
All of the outputs have
now been calculated.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.230
So therefore Shiny is idle.

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.460
Yes, it was.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:38.800
It happened at 11.45,
and it finished at 11.51.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.350
So 0.06 of a second.

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:45.910
So the timeline across the top
does not represent actual time.

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.840
It represents steps taken.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.150
And so you can have
many steps taken

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:52.810
in a very short amount of time.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:54.600
And you can have
hardly any steps

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:58.650
taken in a very long
amount of actual time.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.850
Such as when I go
from this step--

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:05.850
And so as we were
talking about there,

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.820
the time up here
represents the time

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.980
since recording or
starting the application,

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.950
and then the session represents
what Shiny session it

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:15.180
belongs to.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.520
Typically when you
open up a reactlog,

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.490
that session will be isolated
to any global sessions

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:26.380
such as Shiny idle, or any
session that I am running in.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.060
So in this case, I'm running
in the Shiny session 2D7

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:31.560
blah, blah, blah.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:37.210
And between these two steps
about ten seconds had elapsed.

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:39.810
So Shiny was idle at
the one second mark

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:43.020
and then we started changing
the value ten seconds later.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:50.720
Just a quick to show about
switching between idles so

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:58.710
if we click the button here,
we can go back and forth

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.210
between idle sessions.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.710
Most likely that's
something where

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:06.460
you know you've done something
within the application.

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:08.940
The graph doesn't necessarily
change because the app

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:10.020
is fairly small.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:17.570
In the last one that I want to
show in this is the searching.

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:22.830
So you can search for
these labels here.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:28.820
So in this case, I can search
for let's say plot and all

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.950
the things that show up.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.810
Or even capital Plot.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:37.680
These three items all
contain a capital P in plot

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:42.140
and this is a smaller app so
the searching isn't quite as

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:42.710
effective.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:48.220
But if I did the lowercase
plot it's still the same graph

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.610
because the ancestors and
descendants are the same.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.280
Another way to go
around this, I like

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.960
to hit Escape to escape that
searching that was done.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.620
Another fun one is that you
can single click an item.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:06.740
So it kind of shows up while
you move around and do things.

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:09.640
So you can see that
one specifically.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:11.110
Escape to get rid of it.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:14.182
But what I like to do
is to double click.

00:12:14.182 --> 00:12:15.640
And it will kind
of make it bigger.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.260
It'll highlight it,
and it will find

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:20.470
all of its ancestors and
descendants, its whole family

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:21.160
tree.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:23.890
And when you move
back and forth,

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:28.310
it will only be for that
tree that is displayed.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.100
So if there are
other reactives, you

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:33.310
have 1,000 other reactives above
or below that aren't shown,

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:34.840
we'll skip over all of them.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:37.547
So we'll only look at the
reactivity for the sub

00:12:37.547 --> 00:12:39.520
tree that is displayed.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:41.650
And that is really
useful, because when

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.800
you have an app like Gradient.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:52.060
I think on load it has about
2,500 reactive IDs created.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.740
Yeah, it's big.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.870
It's a very big app.

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.930
It's wonderful for testing
by the way, thank you.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.230
And has a lot of
different corner cases.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:05.860
But yeah it's a
very large graph.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:07.360
Very shallow graph, actually.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:09.280
I was very surprised about that.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.730
Not very many dependencies,
something like this,

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.800
like three layers deep.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.290
But useful nonetheless.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:21.440
So let's look at a
more direct example.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.320
Looking at Pythagoras
app, and I like

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:30.040
to look at this one as an app
that people aren't necessarily

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:30.790
familiar with.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.530
Because we know the
Pythagoras Theorem,

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:35.080
we know how to calculate it.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.960
But we can do this
through reactivity

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:44.530
because if I change A or B, then
my output C should also update.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:47.770
That's how the definition
of the triangle is done.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:54.250
And let's look at how we would
calculate Pythagoras theorem.

00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:59.620
So to calculate that, we need
to first calculate A squared.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:01.900
We can label it with A squared.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:04.510
And I highly recommend
you giving anything

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:09.250
that you can a label
because most likely,

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.140
you'll have a
multi-line expression

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.380
and possibly your
first few lines might

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:17.410
be very similar to other
reactive expressions.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:21.880
And so your default label is
not going to be that useful.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:24.230
Instead we know
this is A squared.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:28.000
So I can give it a nice
value of A squared.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.870
This is underutilized but
is very effective when

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.580
viewing the reactlog.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:40.190
So future you will thank you for
your work that you do up front.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:42.880
So we have A squared, calculate.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:45.880
We then have B squared
and we calculate it.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:46.510
Great.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:50.360
Given these two reactive
values we can retrieve them,

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:53.630
add them together
to make C squared.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:55.780
And then finally,
given C squared

00:14:55.780 --> 00:15:01.940
we can take the square root
of it and have our answer, C.

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:04.910
This value is then
displayed via render text

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:09.170
and also a render print just
so that we have more outputs

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:09.830
to look at.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.660
But it could just be done with
a single render print or render

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:13.160
text.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.970
OK so let's run
this application.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:20.910
In the top I have a
reactlog react log enable

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.190
to enable the reactivity.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.790
And then finally I will
call Command fn F 3 just

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:27.990
to open up the viewer.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.730
So let's run the application.

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:36.800
Cool.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:39.560
So this is a fun surprise I
skipped over it in the code.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:45.370
You don't necessarily need
to always open up the viewer.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:50.230
Instead what you can do is add
a UI value and a server value.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:54.940
The UI value is
reactlog module UI.

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:57.850
So it's a UI module that you
can add into your application

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:01.265
just so the reactlog
is inserted in

00:16:01.265 --> 00:16:03.265
and you don't need to
open up in multiple pages.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:09.580
To pair with this, we need to
have the reactlog module server

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:13.720
so that we can update
those values in the UI.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.970
It won't magically
just occur, Shiny

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:19.381
needs to be told that
these things can be done.

00:16:19.381 --> 00:16:22.420
So that's where
that's coming from.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:26.490
So in our application, we
have our A, we have our B,

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.820
and we have our C. Output
C and output C. Two

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:30.750
different outputs.

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:35.350
And the reactlog
looks like this.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:40.230
So we have A to A
squared, B to B squared.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.690
A squared and B squared
makes C squared.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.720
C squared can be
square rooted into C.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:52.290
And C is then stored in the
output, C verbatim and C text.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:55.650
And that matched exactly how
we had in our application.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:01.390
Input A to A
squared, input B to B

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:05.109
squared, A squared and B
squared pointing to C squared,

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:07.089
and the square root
of C squared makes

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:10.869
C. And that matches our labels,
everything is nice and tidy,

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:14.060
and we can see our
images right in the app.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:16.369
Very exciting.

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:20.720
You'll notice that there's this
extra item of a theme counter.

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:24.890
That's for things like Bootstrap
Lib, where we update the theme

00:17:24.890 --> 00:17:26.240
and do things like that.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:27.869
You can feel free to ignore it.

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:29.160
It's not a big deal.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:35.720
And then this extra line here
is the reactlog module UI

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:36.620
and the IFrame.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:40.220
Because this is actually
an IFrame to the reactlog

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:41.840
information.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.240
So we can just feel
free to ignore that.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:46.370
That's not a big deal.

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:57.270
OK so let's look at what
happens when we change this

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.540
to 5 and 12.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.820
We get the 5 12, 13 triangle.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:03.540
Great.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.100
Not too big of a surprise.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:09.540
Let's refresh our reactlog
because more reactivity

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:11.180
has occurred.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.760
And it only loads it
up to the first time

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:17.120
when we did the application
when it was idle.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.390
So we can walk through this
application using our arrow

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:25.370
keys, and when we change
the value A went from 3 to 5

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.370
so all the downstreams
get invalidated.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.470
And they're
invalidating by removing

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:32.630
all of their dependencies
and marking themselves

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.280
as invalidated.

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:36.320
And then they work
their way back up.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:41.900
And B squared was not a
direct descendant of A.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:44.660
So therefore, B squared
will keep its value

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:47.510
but it will just
remove the connection

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:48.920
from B squared to C squared.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:52.800
C squared is actually initiating
that removal of the connection.

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.980
And so B squared it is
going to stay calculated.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.800
And if you were
to ask its value,

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:59.150
it would return immediately.

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:04.160
It would not need to recalculate
because it was not invalidated.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.560
And then once all of those
investigations occur,

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:08.660
Shiny sits there
and goes OK, what

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.520
outputs need to be calculated.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:12.300
And we'll walk
through that again.

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.930
So C verbatim says,
hey, I need a value.

00:19:14.930 --> 00:19:18.830
It captures C. C needs to
work its way up because it

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:19.940
doesn't have a value.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:23.450
A squared works its way up,
gets A, calculates its value.

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:25.790
C squared is now
happy with A squared,

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:28.610
but it also needs the B squared.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:31.560
B squared in this case has
already been calculated,

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.380
so it returns its
value immediately.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:36.820
No time is spent
calculating B squared.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:40.150
I'm up the tree back
down to C verbatim.

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.730
C verbatim is
happy, but we still

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:45.550
have more outputs to calculate.

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:47.410
So we'll move on to C text.

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:51.280
C text will then look at C and
say, hey, I need your value.

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:55.280
C will be like, great,
here's my value, we're done.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.220
There's no more
calculations needed.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:02.150
Shiny has this automatic
caching value already done.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.350
And so if a reactive value
has not been invalidated,

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.630
it will return its
value immediately

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:11.120
and no time is spent
calculating that.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:12.370
And I think that's just great.

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:15.560
Like it's a neat little
trick hidden underneath.

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.790
And if you're to add an output
dynamically, like right now,

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:22.000
and all it needed was the
C value and B squared,

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:23.620
it would be instantaneous.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:28.097
You wouldn't have no extra
overhead, and that is great.

00:20:28.097 --> 00:20:28.930
And then we're done.

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.760
Shiny is idle.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:33.580
But we also then
updated the value to 12

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:35.920
and we ran through the
same process again.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:38.740
But we went up the B branch
instead of the A branch.

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:43.870
Done.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:44.740
Shiny is idle.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:50.330
And then the next
steps actually are

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:54.440
me clicking the Refresh button
and then the IFrame updating.

00:20:59.450 --> 00:21:03.320
When you click the
Refresh button though,

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:07.250
it will always go back to
that first idle location.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:10.580
And the reactlog is append only.

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:11.780
I don't cut it.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.670
I don't do anything special.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.410
I just append on
because I don't want

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:18.660
to hide the truth from
what's there from you.

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.920
So what you can do is
actually mark a time point.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.260
And I find this really
useful because most

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:25.950
likely in your
Shiny application,

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:29.760
you will have to do many
steps to possibly get

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.890
to your bad reactivity.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:35.340
And then you'll say, hey,
I need it mark this time

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.290
point because I know
the next steps that I

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.930
do maybe a little wonky.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:43.290
So to mark a time point,
you can do Command fn, F4.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:46.320
Or a little bit more
reliable, given your browser,

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:48.690
you can do Command,
Shift, fn, and F3.

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:53.670
So very similar to Command, fn,
F3 but you add in the Shift.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:57.160
And what this will do is
it'll mark a time point.

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:00.570
So when we refresh
our application

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.780
it won't load to
where it's first idle.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:08.200
It'll load to the last
marked time point.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.080
So you can have many
marked time points,

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.420
but it'll load the last one.

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:16.470
And this is really nice
because if we go back

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:21.600
to a 3, 4 triangle.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:25.470
Right, so 3, 4, 5,
and then I refresh.

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:30.730
It was loaded up before
refresh and so we can go--

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:35.940
I'm sitting in a
5, 12, 13 triangle.

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:41.930
And if I were to
move forward, this

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:46.370
is now running through and
converting it back to the 3, 4.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:48.900
So right where I last
marked my time point.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.300
So for completeness, I'm going
to do another marked time

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:51.800
point.

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:54.870
And I'm going to
refresh the reactlog.

00:22:54.870 --> 00:22:58.200
So you can see the marked
time point down here.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:03.740
And I can actually jump between
them using the black bar.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:07.560
Because that is a
marked time point.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:11.150
Maybe we should remove
them, unless they're there.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:15.620
But I thought it was to
help show that it's possible

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:17.840
that they could be there.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:20.660
OK so let's look at a
situation where reactlog will

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:23.000
help us debug our reactivity.

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:25.500
We had our Pythagoras
application here.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:28.985
And now let's look
at a broken one.

00:23:28.985 --> 00:23:31.110
So the difference between
Pythagoras and Pythagoras

00:23:31.110 --> 00:23:33.450
broken is not that much.

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:38.610
The app will run and no
errors will be produced.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:39.960
This is actually terrifying.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:41.790
Because this means
it's a logic error

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:44.710
and it is not actually
a Shiny error.

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:46.530
So let's go ahead and
run the application

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:50.110
with our reactlog enabled.

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:52.180
So we're running
your application.

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:54.480
Great.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:58.710
So what is going on here?

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:01.380
Our C value is 4.2.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:05.490
It is not a 3, 4, 5 triangle.

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:10.680
And C squared is listening
to B squared sometimes

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.930
because there's a
connection being made.

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:14.715
But it's not solid.

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:19.310
And what?

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:21.664
B squared is listening to A?

00:24:21.664 --> 00:24:23.430
This is weird.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:30.110
So let's change it
to a 5 and a 12.

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:32.180
Still 7.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:34.190
OK.

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:37.220
So odd.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:43.460
All right so let's look
had this application.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.620
So did actually it change?

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:51.600
Yeah, if I change
B nothing happens.

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.480
So this isn't good either.

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:58.680
So walking through the reactlog,
let's see what happens here.

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:02.270
So this is when I was changing
it into that 3, 4, 5 triangle.

00:25:02.270 --> 00:25:04.610
So A changed into--

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:05.900
Or a 5, 12, 13.

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:08.980
So A changed into 5, great.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:11.330
A is telling B to invalidate.

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:13.970
That seems odd.

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:15.380
So there it is.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.720
Input A is B squared.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:20.300
We should switch that.

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:22.760
So that is now B.

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:26.270
So personally, that's
a better logic change.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:30.630
Let's just rerun the
application here.

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:31.910
So we'll run app again.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:40.970
OK things are looking better.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:43.420
Let's switch this to a 5.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:46.770
And then we switch this to a 12.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:49.500
And B is still broken.

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:50.520
That's not good.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:55.800
But we can see that B squared
is taking an input from B.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:57.540
So that seems better.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:25:59.860
So we're doing better there.

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:04.150
So let's walk through
that adjustment.

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:08.470
So given that adjustment,
A turned into 5,

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:12.450
A squared, C squared,
OK everything's good.

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:13.440
Seems right.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.690
Output C verbatim
is recalculating

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:17.940
getting its A squared.

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:24.030
And C squared is isolating
on B squared, odd.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:26.130
And moving on.

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:31.080
OK so that's how that connection
was made at some point.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:34.110
But it's not
connected right now.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:36.240
And I guess that's
why it makes sense

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:39.330
that when B changes its value
all the way up to anything,

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:42.540
like 20, C is not changing.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:49.650
So isolate value here.

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:52.330
We remove that B squared.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:53.610
Great.

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:55.260
So we'll save it.

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:59.670
Let's kill the application
because we made a known logic

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.770
change that's better.

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:06.840
And things I believe
will be good.

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:10.590
So 5 and 12.

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:13.140
5, 12, 13 triangle.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:17.970
And if we refresh
our reactlog, we

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:21.120
can see that A
makes its updates.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:26.370
And we can also see
that we change B

00:27:26.370 --> 00:27:28.710
and it'll make its updates.

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:30.640
And C was actually
updated twice,

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:33.750
which is what we want for
all the changes of A and B

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:37.340
and Shiny's idle.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:40.360
So that's how we can
use it to help debug.

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:43.330
And it's helped
debug logic changes.

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.900
And this works really well when
your application is very large.

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.660
Because this can work
with a couple of thousand

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.970
of these different
reactives, no problem.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:01.040
Just to show a
bigger application,

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:04.130
I'd like to look at
the cranwhales demo app

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:06.600
that Joe showed at
one of his keynotes.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:15.530
So again, we're going to make
sure that reactlog is enabled.

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:20.720
We're going to run the
GitHub one application.

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:25.100
And this application shows
the top end downloaders

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:28.910
of all of cran that's
hosted by RStudio.

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:31.880
And it's usually the
80-20 rule, where

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:37.970
like six people are responsible
for a lot more than 20%

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:41.617
or 80% of the traffic, you
know depending on the day.

00:28:41.617 --> 00:28:42.950
So we're thinking about it here.

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:49.200
And downloading that data.

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.870
Great, all right, so
the data has shown up.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:56.040
We have some numbers up at the
top of the bandwidth consumed,

00:28:56.040 --> 00:28:58.800
the files downloaded,
the unique downloaders.

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:03.270
So out of the 120,000
unique downloaders,

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:08.500
the green bars are
represented by 6 people.

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:10.230
So these are the whales.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:12.690
And so it's the cranwhales.

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:17.670
And that's almost 50%
by those six people.

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:23.680
The biggest whales are
given random names.

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:28.170
And so The Full Puppy,
he's the biggest whale.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:30.210
And by hours, actually
very interesting,

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:32.070
there's different behaviors.

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:35.700
Full Puppy looks kind of
like internet traffic.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:38.370
The Glamorous Parakeet,
pretty consistent.

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:42.870
And The Glamorous Fish,
very spiked behavior.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:46.883
And Immense Zebra
looks very constant.

