00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:23.340
One thing to look
for with reactlog,

00:35:23.340 --> 00:35:25.950
because you'll find some
shocking behaviors every once

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:28.200
in a while--

00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:32.580
and this is what Joe referred
to as, in one of his talks,

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:37.850
as an anti-pattern --and
basically what happens

00:35:37.850 --> 00:35:42.350
is if you observe on something,
like an input button,

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:47.750
and you use a render
method inside an observer,

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:49.940
this is typically bad.

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:51.740
There are use cases.

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:55.040
Like you add a new tab where
it has completely independent

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:56.120
reactivity.

00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:58.410
Cool, you should do this.

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:02.800
But in this case, if
I just want to render

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:06.070
a plot to bad pattern,
something that's a static name,

00:36:06.070 --> 00:36:07.870
and this expression
will actually

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:10.450
be evaluated many
times, that is actually

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:13.810
making new render plots over
and over and over again.

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:15.440
And that is bad.

00:36:15.440 --> 00:36:19.630
Instead, what we should
do, is calculate some data

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:22.570
such as a click count.

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:26.320
And call render plot
and wait until the input

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:27.760
button has been clicked.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:31.990
So I require the input
button to be not zero,

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:33.970
and then I'll give
you the input button.

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:36.190
That was the goal of this thing.

00:36:36.190 --> 00:36:39.820
Such as when the button is
clicked, render the plot.

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:42.610
So instead we can
say, make a reactive

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:45.790
on input button that says you
require it to be something

00:36:45.790 --> 00:36:47.770
truthy, or non-zero.

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:49.450
You'll have click
count, and then we

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:53.130
render the plot given
the click count.

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:55.500
If you really wanted to
be dynamic and random

00:36:55.500 --> 00:37:00.000
you could do it using
a dynamic output ID.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:01.500
That's OK.

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:03.720
That's kind of like
inserting a new tab

00:37:03.720 --> 00:37:06.150
or doing something
that has completely

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:08.400
independent reactive logic.

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:11.340
But the typical use case
of click the button,

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:14.670
render the plot, this is bad.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:17.760
And we'll see it in
reactlog log plain as day.

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.220
So let's click the button.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.390
OK, click the button.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:22.950
OK we got some new plots.

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:29.820
Let's actually take a
look at this reactlog.

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:31.740
And you can see that
the reactlog will get

00:37:31.740 --> 00:37:34.920
taller, and taller, and taller.

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:40.860
And this is because we will have
more and more outputs of output

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:43.310
dollar bad pattern.

00:37:43.310 --> 00:37:46.045
So let's move forward
in the reactlog.

00:37:48.940 --> 00:37:53.730
So we can see that this
output bad pattern will

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:58.983
be calculated at some point.

00:37:58.983 --> 00:38:00.900
And then it's finally
calculated there, great.

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:03.570
You don't really notice
it off of one click.

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:09.540
But what if we clicked it one,
two, three, four, five times.

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:10.740
And now let's refresh.

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:15.900
Let's mark this time point.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:17.200
Marked, great.

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:20.580
And let's refresh
our application.

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:24.390
Now our reactlog
is a lot taller.

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:27.990
And if we zoom in, we can
see output bad pattern,

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:31.790
output bad pattern,
output bad pattern.

00:38:31.790 --> 00:38:35.030
There's four of
these, five of these.

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:38.810
And so I can search for that.

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:41.360
And they're all making
different plot objects,

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:44.720
and they're all listening
to the same inputs.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:46.660
So this is not good.

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:50.800
And none of the output bad
patterns exist at any one time.

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:53.780
Like they're not
coexisting at any one time.

00:38:53.780 --> 00:38:57.010
And so this is an
anti-pattern and something

00:38:57.010 --> 00:38:59.440
to avoid if you see it.

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:05.530
It's not the worst but
it will cause issues

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:07.150
after hours, and
hours, and hours.

